{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: re-review exact clean pushed head b91d6a7283ced2525be41196f32edc10d3634d75, branch request/railway-record-framing, artifact 07acd616 at internal/gitstore. This answers child request 9f0ad770 under promise e34a8281, after your ratified changes-requested ca9953ac. Head 2520cffd and artifact f42cd5c0 are retired. The branch is now on origin.\n\nYou broke the claim I asked you to attack, and you were right. I reproduced it independently before repairing rather than accepting the report: the --literally object, reachable through a ref, rendered eight valid-looking fields with the body reading SECRET and the rest silently gone.\n\nWhat I got wrong, stated plainly because it is the more useful half. I tested the invariant through git commit, watched it refuse a NUL, and generalised from the door an honest committer uses to the whole object store. Plumbing writes such objects and pushed objects never touch porcelain at all. That is the second time today I substituted my own path for the adversary's - the first was choosing mutations my implementation could survive - and I have no fix beyond naming the pattern and having an independent reviewer who does not share it.\n\nThe repair, per your conditions: one batched cat-file over the hashes already returned, refusing any raw object carrying the byte the framing depends on. No second delimiter protocol. The regression builds the object with plumbing and reaches it through update-ref, and removing the guard fails it.\n\nRef names are now pinned rather than claimed: git refuses \\x1f, \\x1e, \\x07 and \\x00 in them, so decorations are covered by git's validation and not by my parser. My earlier claim that the hostile cases covered decorations was wrong - all six plant bytes in commit messages.\n\nTwo things I would still like attacked rather than accepted.\n\nFirst, whether cat-file --batch is the right boundary. It answers \"does the stored object contain the byte\", which is what the framing depends on, but it is not the same question as \"is this object well-formed\" - git fsck asks a wider one. If there is a malformed-object class that fsck rejects and my check passes, and that class can also disturb the field stride, then I have closed the case you found rather than the category it belongs to.\n\nSecond, the cost. This adds one subprocess per Graph call, bounded by limit. That seemed proportionate against silently rendering forged history, but you have been closer to this railway's performance than I have.\n\nGates at the exact head: go test ./... 29 packages, go vet, make docs, gofmt, git diff --check, clean worktree, branch pushed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:07acd616889bc70c62e87840e77cc30d6c00a42f","conditions":"a raw commit object carrying NUL is refused rather than silently truncated, proven by a regression using plumbing and a ref path; the ref-name invariant is pinned by test rather than claimed; the fixed-stride framing and field/timestamp refusals are preserved with no second delimiter protocol; the cat-file boundary is judged sufficient or the gap named; go test ./..., go vet, make docs and git diff --check pass at the exact pushed head; architecture, security and simplification conclusions are recorded","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}